%I A399793 #18 Sep 17 2026 16:54:04
%S A399793 0,1,2,2,3,4,4,5,5,5,6,6,7,7,7,8,8,9,9,10,10,10,10,11,11,13,13,14,14,
%T A399793 14,14
%N A399793 a(n) is the largest size of a subset S of {1, ..., n} such that there does not exist a function S -> {-1,1} satisfying |Sum_{x in S} f(x)| <= 1.
%C A399793 A set S consisting of positive integers is called good if there exists a function S -> {-1,1} satisfying |Sum_{x in S} f(x)| <= 1. Otherwise it is called bad.
%C A399793 S is a good set if and only if its subset-sum set contains floor(T/2), where T is the sum of elements of S.
%C A399793 a(n) is the largest size of a bad subset of {1, ..., n}.
%C A399793 Problem 4 of 2021 China National High School Mathematics League Second Round asks to find the smallest real number c such that a(n) <= c*n for all n. The answer is 2/3, and the upper bound comes from a(6) = 4.
%H A399793 Art of Problem Solving, <a href="https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h2669945p23135852">2021 China National High School Mathematics League Second Round, Problem 4</a>
%H A399793 Bingyuan Wang, <a href="http://www.nsmath.cn/filedownload/539123">The best possible result of 2021 China National High School Mathematics League Second Round Problem 4 for sufficiently large n (in Chinese)</a>, New Star Students' Column.
%F A399793 a(n) >= floor(n/2) if n == 2, 3, 4, 5 (mod 8);
%F A399793 a(n) >= floor(n/2) - 1 if n == 0, 1, 6, 7 (mod 8).
%F A399793 The equality is achieved when n >= 564.
%F A399793 Conjecture: The equality is achieved when n >= 13.
%F A399793 For n = 8*k + 2 or 8*k + 3, S = {2, 4, ..., 8*k + 2} is a bad set, so a(n) >= 4*k + 1;
%F A399793 For n = 8*k + 4, 8*k + 5, 8*k + 6 or 8*k + 7, S = {2, 4, ..., 8*k + 4} is a bad set, so a(n) >= 4*k + 2;
%F A399793 For n = 8*k + 8 or 8*k + 9, S = {2, 4, ..., 8*k + 8} \ {8*k + 6} is a bad set, so a(n) >= 4*k + 3.
%e A399793 For n = 6, a(6) = 4 because {1, 4, 5, 6} is a bad set, while every 5-subset of {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} is a good set.
%Y A399793 Cf. A181176, A386820.
